{#
  /llms.txt — the site described in markdown, for a language model reading it.

  The llmstxt.org convention: an H1 name, a blockquote summary, optional prose, then H2
  sections of `- [Title](url): description`. A model landing here reads this instead of
  crawling forty pages and guessing which matter.

  GENERATED, NOT WRITTEN. Every line comes from the CMS, so publishing a case study adds
  it and unpublishing removes it. A hand-written file at the webroot would be accurate
  for a fortnight and quietly wrong after — worse than having none, because it would
  feed a model stale claims about the work.

  AUTOESCAPE OFF, and it has to be: this is a text document, not HTML. With escaping on,
  "Design & Development" came out as "Design &amp; Development". There is no injection
  risk to trade against — the response is text/plain, so nothing in it is ever parsed as
  markup.

  text/plain rather than text/markdown: the latter isn't a registered type, so browsers
  download it instead of showing it. Plain text opens in a tab, which is what anyone
  checking this wants.

  Whitespace control throughout — the output IS the document, so a stray newline from a
  `{% set %}` is a blank line in the markdown.
#}
{%- header "Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8" -%}
{%- set about = craft.entries().section('about').status('live').one() -%}
{%- set caseStudyIndex = craft.entries().section('caseStudy').status('live').one() -%}
{%- set notesIndex = craft.entries().section('note').status('live').one() -%}
{%- set contact = craft.entries().section('contact').status('live').one() -%}
{%- set tools = craft.entries().section('tools').status('live').all() -%}
{%- set studies = craft.entries().section('caseStudies').status('live').all() -%}
{%- set notes = craft.entries().section('notes').status('live').orderBy('postDate DESC').limit(10).all() -%}
{%- set pages = craft.entries().section('pages').status('live').all() -%}
{#- ONE LINE, cleaned. Summaries are multi-paragraph and some are rich text, but a
    description in this format has to be a single line — so: strip tags, take the first
    paragraph, flatten any remaining newlines. -#}
{#- The autoescape has to be INSIDE the macro. A Twig macro inherits the escaping
    context where it is DEFINED, not where it is called — so with only the outer block
    turned off, everything this returned still came back escaped and apostrophes landed
    as `&#039;` in a plain-text file. The stored values are clean; the escaping was
    entirely self-inflicted. -#}
{%- macro line(text) -%}
    {%- autoescape false -%}
        {{- (text ?? '')|striptags|split("\n\n")|first|trim|replace({"\n": ' ', "\r": ''}) -}}
    {%- endautoescape -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
{%- import _self as f -%}
{% autoescape false %}
# {{ siteName }}

> {{ f.line(about.summary ?? '') ?: 'Design, development and maintenance of digital products.' }}

{{ f.line(caseStudyIndex.summary ?? '') }}

This site has an assistant on the homepage that answers questions about the work in Jon's
own voice. Everything it draws on is published here, so the pages below are the source
rather than a summary of it.

## Start here

- [Home]({{ siteUrl() }}): the front door, and the assistant — ask it anything about the work.
{% if about %}- [{{ about.title }}]({{ about.url }}): {{ f.line(about.summary ?? '') }}
{% endif %}
{%- if caseStudyIndex %}- [{{ caseStudyIndex.title }}]({{ caseStudyIndex.url }}): {{ f.line(caseStudyIndex.summary ?? '') }}
{% endif %}
{%- if contact %}- [{{ contact.title }}]({{ contact.url }}): how to get in touch.
{% endif %}
{#- THE TOOLS SIT IN "START HERE" rather than in a section of their own, because a free
    report is the one thing on this site a stranger can be given, and a model answering
    "who can look at my homepage?" should meet it in the first handful of lines rather
    than below seven case studies. The note announcing the tool was already listed under
    Notes while the tool itself was nowhere — so anything reading this file found the
    announcement and never the page it announced. -#}
{%- for tool in tools %}- [{{ tool.title }}]({{ tool.url }}): {{ f.line(tool.summary ?? '') }}
{% endfor %}

{% if studies|length %}## Case studies

{% for study in studies -%}
{#- seoMetaDescription first: it is already a single sentence written to describe the
    piece. caseSummary is CKEditor rich text and reads as the opening of the page rather
    than a description of it, so it is the fallback rather than the first choice. -#}
{%- set s = f.line(study.seoMetaDescription ?? '') ?: f.line(study.caseSummary ?? '') -%}
- [{{ study.title }}]({{ study.url }}){% if s %}: {{ s }}{% endif %}

{% endfor %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if notes|length %}

## Notes

{{ f.line(notesIndex.summary ?? '') }}

{% for note in notes -%}
{%- set s = f.line(note.summary ?? '') ?: f.line(note.seoMetaDescription ?? '') -%}
- [{{ note.title }}]({{ note.url }}){% if s %}: {{ s }}{% endif %}

{% endfor -%}
{%- if notesIndex %}- [All notes]({{ notesIndex.url }}): the full archive, by year and by topic.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}

## Optional

{# "Optional" is the convention's own heading for what a model may skip when short of
    context. Policies belong there — they matter, but they are not what anyone is asking
    about. The AI policy is the one most worth reading, and it comes first by virtue of
    being the only page in that section.

    This comment sits BELOW its heading on purpose. Between the heading and the list
    above it, its own `{#-` trim marker ate the blank line separating the two sections —
    and every attempt to add one back was eaten the same way. Below, it is harmless. -#}
{% for page in pages -%}
{%- set s = f.line(page.summary ?? '') ?: f.line(page.seoMetaDescription ?? '') -%}
- [{{ page.title }}]({{ page.url }}){% if s %}: {{ s }}{% endif %}

{% endfor -%}
- [Notes feed]({{ siteUrl('notes.rss') }}): RSS, the 20 most recent notes.
{% endautoescape %}
